  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Golden Gilled Bolete is a distinctive fungus characterized by its bright yellow gill-like structures (lamellae) rather than typical bolete pores, making it unique within the bolete family. Found throughout New England, this mushroom is a mycorrhizal species that forms relationships with hardwood trees.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Yellow Gills/Lamellae",
      "description": "Bright yellow, blade-like gill structures that run from the stem to the cap edge, distinguishing it from typical pored boletes",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Color",
      "description": "Golden-brown to reddish-brown cap that is smooth and convex when young, flattening with age",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Features",
      "description": "Yellow stem with red or pink coloration near the base, often becoming darker toward the bottom",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This species thrives in mixed hardwood forests throughout New England, typically appearing in summer and fall months under oak, beech, and other deciduous trees.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The Golden Gilled Bolete's gill-like structures represent an evolutionary bridge between traditional gilled mushrooms and pored boletes, making it scientifically fascinating to mycologists.",
